GitHub's own commit-signing keys ("web-flow"), fetched from
https://github.com/web-flow.gpg on 2026-09-12.

GitHub signs with these whenever a commit is created by GitHub itself rather
than on a participant's machine: the "Merge pull request" / "Squash and merge"
buttons, and edits made in the web UI. A team using pull requests -- the
ordinary GitHub workflow -- will have several of these in its history.

Without them, every one of those merges resolves to UNKNOWN_KEY and hard-flags
an honest team. They are imported alongside the roster so the signature can be
verified and attributed to the platform, NOT to a participant: a commit signed
by one of these is reported as PLATFORM_KEY and never counts as team authorship.

Trusting these fingerprints is safe in a way that trusting a participant's key
would not be: no participant holds GitHub's private key, so nobody can forge a
signature into this bucket.

  5DE3E0509C47EA3CF04A42D34AEE18F83AFDEB23  GitHub (web-flow commit signing)
  968479A1AFF927E37D1A566BB5690EEEBB952194  GitHub

